@meta
id: 10-19
title: Paragraphs and sentences
chapter: 10
type: lesson
skills: obj-paragraph, inner-vs-around
par: 30
minutes: 6
vscode: click at the start, scroll, shift-click at the end, delete, tidy the blank lines
open: refunds.md

@brief
Prose has objects too. `ip` is a paragraph — a run of lines with no blank
line in it — and `ap` is that paragraph plus the blank line that follows
it, which is what keeps a document's spacing correct when you remove
something from the middle of it.

`is` and `as` are the same idea one level down: a sentence, ending at a
`.`, `!` or `?`. A sentence happily spans a line break, which is the whole
reason to have the object. Deleting a sentence that starts in the middle
of one line and ends in the middle of the next is a genuinely annoying
edit by hand.

This doc went stale when the nightly batch job was switched off. Three
things have to go:

  - the sentence in the opening paragraph about the batch job having
    handled refunds
  - the entire `## Legacy batch job` section, heading and paragraph both
  - the last paragraph, about the error code that no longer exists

What is left should read normally: one space between sentences, one blank
line between sections, no gap left at the end.

@keys
| `dap` | delete a paragraph and the blank line after it |
| `dip` | the paragraph only, blank line left behind |
| `d2ap` | two paragraphs — a heading counts as one |
| `das` | delete a sentence, wherever it starts and ends |
| `yip` | yank a paragraph |

@hints
A markdown heading with a blank line under it is a paragraph in vim's
sense: one run of non-blank lines. The section is therefore two of them.
--
`das` for the sentence, `dap` for a paragraph. Objects take counts, so the
heading and its paragraph can go in one command.
--
`fR` puts you on the start of the stale sentence. For the section, put
the cursor on the heading and use a count of two with `ap`.

@solution
    3j fR das
    2j d2ap
    4j dap
    :wq<Enter>

Twenty-two keystrokes to remove a sentence, a section and a paragraph
from a document, with the blank lines coming out right on their own.

`as` is the object that surprises people, because vim's idea of a sentence
predates markdown by decades and is purely mechanical: it ends at `.`, `!`
or `?` followed by a space, a tab or the end of the line. It does not know
that `e.g.` is not the end of a sentence, and it does not care that your
paragraph is hard-wrapped at 80 columns. Within those limits it is exact,
and it saves you from the worst kind of manual selection — the one that
crosses a line break.

`ap` versus `ip` is the same trailing-whitespace rule as `aw` and `a"`,
scaled up: `ap` takes the blank line after the paragraph, or the one
before it if the paragraph is the last thing in the file. That fallback is
why the end of this document came out with no trailing gap.

`yip` is the other half you will use constantly — yank a paragraph, put it
somewhere else. Reordering a document is `yip`, move, `p`.

@start refunds.md
# Refunds

Refunds are processed by the payment service. A refund is only attempted
when the order is inside the 30 day window. Refunds were previously handled
by the legacy batch job. Outside the window the order is still cancelled.

## Legacy batch job

The nightly batch job reconciled refunds against the provider statement.
It was switched off in March and the cron entry has been removed with it.

## Errors

A failed refund raises REFUND_FAILED and is reported to Sentry.

Refunds queued by the batch job raised BATCH_REFUND_FAILED instead. That
code was removed from the error registry in the same release.

